worked base 10, like we do. So they had symbols;&nbsp;
and I'm going to attempt to draw some hieroglyphs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:17.680
So their symbol for the number 1 is this&nbsp;
- groundbreaking - it's called a single stroke.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:24.120
So if they wanted to say 2, they combine two of&nbsp;
these. Um for 10 in hieroglyphs they have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:30.240
which I believe is called a cattle hobble, which I&nbsp;
think was used- you maybe put it around cattle legs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.480
to slow them down. I looked into it, don't really&nbsp;
know what that does, but this is a 10 which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.280
a far more interesting use of it. For 100 - and now&nbsp;
I'm almost definitely going to get this the wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.760
way around - but I think it's this way and it's a&nbsp;
coil of rope. I'm pretty sure it's that way round,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:52.160
someone will correct me if it's not. For 1,000 this&nbsp;
is when it gets slightly more complicated, and I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:01:01.080
got to use my art GCSE; it is the water lily. For&nbsp;
10,000, a bent finger, that looks vaguely like this,&nbsp;

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:07.320
and that's a bent finger. Okay, bent finger. 100,000?&nbsp;
Obviously whenever I think of 100,000 what I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:14.280
of is tadpoles; so this is a tadpole. And now&nbsp;
finally a million, and this really is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:23.160
putting my art skills to use. A million is Heh. Now Heh&nbsp;
is the personification of eternity or infinity. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:29.440
a million also kind of just means an eternal or&nbsp;
infinite amount, which isn't actually unheard of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:36.160
in cultures that we have things like this. Umm&nbsp;
some people believe that 1,001 Arabian nights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.760
actually is meant to mean infinity and a bit more.&nbsp;
So the idea of there being a very large number,&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:49.800
meaning that number or infinity, is actually not&nbsp;
an uncommon idea at all. So these are the Egyptian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:56.280
hieroglyphs for powers of 10; and if you wanted to,&nbsp;
maybe say you wanted to write I don't know 23, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:02.040
you would do is you would write two 10s and a 3.
And you just combine them like that. So now on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:08.600
to fractions; um I'm going to be talking about - I'm&nbsp;
getting technical here - earlier Egyptian fractions.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:14.360
So there is actually evidence that fractions were&nbsp;
done slightly different later on, there's a famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:19.360
Papyrus called the Rhind Mathemathical Papyrus.
What we're going to be talking about is earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:26.640
um when fractions were all done by having a mouth&nbsp;
symbol like this and then a number underneath. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:33.640
you might say this, and what this would be is &nbsp;
a tenth, because it's the mouth symbol over 10. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:40.280
with this we can only get numbers- fractions with&nbsp;
1 in the numerator. In fact there are only two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:49.280
exceptions um to this; so we can have 2/3, which&nbsp;
is like this, and then there is also 3/4 and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:56.560
half for some reason is different. But aside from&nbsp;
that, all others are of the form 1 / n. So it begs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.360
a question, how is this useful? What happens if we&nbsp;
have another another fraction? What happens if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:09.480
have say 5/6? Well 5/6 is going to be just a&nbsp;
1/2 plus a 1/3, so actually we can still do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.960
this. Now with Egyptian fractions, which is what&nbsp;
these are called, all the denominators have to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:24.120
different. Should we try another one? 15/36 is going&nbsp;
to be a 1/3 is 12/36, and that leaves 3/36 which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:30.920
equal to a 1/12. So so far looks like we can do it.
The question is, can we always do it? So for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:36.200
I want to introduce you to the greedy algorithm.&nbsp;
We've got our fraction p over q, and we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.880
to say p is not 1 - because we know if p is 1
we can do it, we'll just write it as is. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:48.560
greedy algorithm looks at the largest 1/n such&nbsp;
that 1/n is less than p/q. And we start by just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:54.480
taking away this 1/n. What do we know about this?
This is going to be positive, because we know this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:03.160
is less than this. So we'll end up with pn - q&nbsp;
over qn which is positive, okay? We can actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:08.680
write an expression for n as well, we could write&nbsp;
n is the ceiling function of q over p, so it means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:15.760
it's the smallest integer above this fraction. Ah!&nbsp;
We haven't actually used the fact that 1/n is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:21.800
the greatest for which this is true. Cos what this&nbsp;
means is that p/q is also going to be less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:28.840
1/(n-1). So we've said that this is going to be&nbsp;
the smallest such n or the largest such 1/n,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:36.880
they're the same thing, that's less than this, hence&nbsp;
we have this inequality too. Now from this we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:44.120
multiply this out and then we get that for example&nbsp;
p times (n-1) is less than q. And if we substitute this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:53.880
in we get that pn - q is strictly less than&nbsp;
p - and we already know it's positive remember. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.400
we've started with p/q, we've taken away 1/n,
 and now we've ended up with a new fraction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:05.720
we'll call it p1/q1 such that p1 is less&nbsp;
than p. Now if p1 is 1 we're done, because then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:13.960
we could just say that p/q is 1/n plus p1/q1.
 And if p1 is 1 we're done. If not, we just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:19.720
repeat this process. So we can then say that, okay&nbsp;
let's find the largest such 1/n, the smallest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:27.800
n, that we get p1/q1 it's greater than 1
over let's call it n1 and less than this - and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:38.160
just repeat. And then we'll end up with p1/q1&nbsp;
take 1/ n1 = p2/q2. And again we'll have, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:48.660
that 0 is less than p2 is less than p1. We can&nbsp;
substitute back in, get p over q is 1/n + 1/n1. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:58.440
Plus then this will be p2/q2. And again if&nbsp;
p2 is 1 we're done - if not we repeat it again. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:02.520
So this is why it's called an algorithm, it's&nbsp;
a series of steps. And what we're going to end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:10.880
up having is 0 is less than pr for some r is&nbsp;
less than - and it just keeps going - less than p. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:16.560
these are all integers, these are all positive&nbsp;
numbers. We can't have it going down infinitely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.360
one of these is eventually- we're going to have to&nbsp;
hit 1. And the way we've constructed these qs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.560
they're all going to be different as well. Hence&nbsp;
this process is eventually going to terminate.&nbsp;

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:31.440
But yeah eventually we're going to get a pr that&nbsp;
equals 1, so we're going to have p/q is

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:39.920
1/n + 1/n1 plus plus 1 over...then qr. And we're going&nbsp;
to be done. So eventually this process does stop,

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.080
so this greedy algorithm means that there is&nbsp;
always an Egyptian fraction. Okay, there's one final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:49.640
cool thing that I want to share with you. Because&nbsp;
here you remember we we used a greedy algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:56.040
where we took the biggest 1/n so n was n was&nbsp;
this value. Now if we slightly change this, if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:06.480
slightly cheat, and instead use the n is q/p&nbsp;
+ 1 - and I'm going to show you this for 1. So 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:14.840
equals- well here q/p, it's 1 over 1. So &nbsp;
q/p is going to be 1 + 1 is 2, so that's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:21.520
half. And then what we're going to do with our half,&nbsp;
this is our new p/q. So q/p, 2 over 1 is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:30.000
2, 2 over 1 + 1 is 3. So it's a 1/2 plus a 1/3.
What remains is 1/6. And we can keep following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:41.920
this algorithm we get a 1/2 plus 1/3 plus- it'd be a&nbsp;
1/7 + 1/43 + .... and so on. This is the cool thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:47.720
as well, because here if at any point we truncate
it it gives the Egyptian fraction closest to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:54.200
1 for that many that many terms. So 1/2  is&nbsp;
the Egyptian fraction with one term closest to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.200 --> 00:08:02.520
1. 1/2 plus 1/3 is the Egyptian fraction&nbsp;
with two terms closest to 1. 1/2 plus 1/3

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:08.980
plus 1/7 is the Egyptian fraction with three&nbsp;
terms closest to 1 - and I just think it's really cool. 

